# Text files are stored and checked out with LF line endings on every platform,
# so the bytes a Linux CI runner reads are the bytes a Windows editor wrote.
* text=auto eol=lf
*.md     text eol=lf
*.ttl    text eol=lf
*.json   text eol=lf
*.jsonld text eol=lf
*.yaml   text eol=lf
*.yml    text eol=lf
*.py     text eol=lf
*.xml    text eol=lf
*.xsd    text eol=lf
*.csv    text eol=lf
*.sparql text eol=lf
*.rq     text eol=lf

# The files under fixtures/synthetic-adapter/ are held to digests recorded in
# that package's crate, and the rules above are what makes those digests
# reproducible: LF on every platform means the bytes a Linux CI runner hashes are
# the bytes a Windows editor wrote. An adapter package that carries a byte-for-
# byte copy of a publisher's file needs a stricter section than this one, marking
# those paths so nothing normalises them at all; adapter/fixtures/README.md
# says why, and the pilot adapter's .gitattributes is the worked example.
